# Cells where the two Windows references disagree with each other, so there is
# no single answer to match. Passed to font_census.py alongside
# fallback.accept, and only on a CEF run, so the same cells stay flagged if
# they ever appear on an Electron pair.
#
# A Windows Electron (Chromium 150) and a Windows CEF (Chromium 144) on the
# same machine, same fonts and the same DWriteCore shim answer these three
# differently:
#
#   U+1CFA  Electron: Nirmala UI       CEF: Sans Serif Collection
#   U+A70D  Electron: Segoe UI         CEF: Sans Serif Collection
#   U+A832  Electron: Nirmala UI       CEF: Sans Serif Collection
#
# DWriteCore has no opinion on any of the three: dwrite_fallback_probe reports
# no mapped font for all of them under every language. The divergence is in
# Chromium's own path for a character its fallback returns nothing for.
# Chromium 150 lands on the script-appropriate family, which is what the Linux
# side answers; Chromium 144 lands on Sans Serif Collection, the pan-Unicode
# variable font.
#
# Matching CEF would mean answering Sans Serif Collection for three codepoints
# gated on the embedder's Chromium version, and regressing the Electron pair
# that currently matches.
U+1CFA
U+A70D
U+A832
